 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Geometry Conflict:** If the inpainted geometry violates the visible geometry from reference views, it causes artifacts. The erasing step mitigates this by removing conflicting regions from supervision [S1].
-*   **Depth Estimation Error:** The initial depth estimation must be reasonably accurate to generate valid trajectories and detect invisible regions correctly [S1].
-*   **Stable Diffusion FOV:** The pre-trained Stable Diffusion model is trained on perspective images, which may affect inpainting quality for panoramic inputs [S1].
+*   **Depth Estimation Error:** The initial depth estimation from a single panorama is critical. Errors here propagate to trajectory generation and warping [S1].
+*   **Geometry Conflict:** Inpainting with Stable Diffusion may generate plausible but geometrically inconsistent structures. The erasing mechanism is essential to prevent these from corrupting the NeRF [S1].
+*   **Warping Accuracy:** The warping function relies on accurate camera poses and depth. Misalignment leads to false positives in conflict detection [S1].
+*   **Convergence:** The method relies on iterative fine-tuning until convergence for each new view. Insufficient iterations may leave artifacts [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Collaborative RGBD Inpainting
-A sub-method that uses Stable Diffusion for RGB inpainting and a depth estimator for depth stitching to fill invisible regions [S1]. It is chosen when generative completion of missing views is required.
+Uses Stable Diffusion for RGB and a depth estimator for depth to fill invisible regions. It differs from standard inpainting by enforcing RGBD consistency. [Link to [Collaborative RGBD Inpainting](12495-collaborative-rgbd-inpainting.md) if available, otherwise link to parent].
 
-### Single-View NeRF Training
-The initial step of training a NeRF from a single panorama and its estimated depth [S1]. It is the foundation for the progressive expansion.
+### Single-View NeRF Initialization
+The base model trained on a single panorama with estimated depth. [Link to [Single-View NeRF](12496-single-view-nerf.md) if available].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training a scene takes about 2 hours on a single Nvidia V100 GPU [S1]. The complexity scales with the number of iterations and the number of reference views, as each iteration involves rendering, warping, inpainting, and fine-tuning [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Uses Instant-NGP with a hash table size of $2^{19}$ and 16 levels of feature vectors of channel size 2 [S1]. Training requires 32G memory on the GPU [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Effectively avoids geometry conflicts between visible and invisible regions, allowing for the reconstruction of panoramic NeRFs from a single input [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the initial depth estimation and the generative inpainting model. The progressive nature may accumulate errors if conflicts are not properly erased [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When only a single panorama is available and a full 360-degree NeRF is needed.
-*   **When not to use:** When multiple views are available, as other methods may be more efficient and accurate.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training a scene takes about 2 hours on a single Nvidia V100 GPU [S1]. The process is iterative, involving 32 new views, each requiring 2,000 iterations of fine-tuning plus initial 10,000 iterations [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Uses Instant-NGP with a hash table size of $2^{19}$ and 16 levels of feature vectors of channel size 2 [S1]. Requires 32G memory on the GPU [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** The method scales by adding more novel views (up to 32 in the reported experiments) to expand the panorama [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Effectively avoids geometry conflicts between visible and invisible regions, allowing for coherent panoramic reconstruction from a single view [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the initial depth estimator and the generative inpainting model. Performance may degrade on in-the-wild data if the depth estimation is poor [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When only a single panorama is available and a full 360-degree NeRF is required [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When multi-view inputs are available, as other methods may be more efficient and accurate [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in the paper "PERF: Panoramic Neural Radiance Field From a Single Panorama" [S1]. It addresses the challenge of reconstructing panoramic NeRFs from a single input, a problem that is ill-posed without additional constraints or generative priors. The method builds on the Instant-NGP representation [S1] and uses Stable Diffusion [S1] for inpainting.
+*   **Origin:** Proposed in the paper "PERF: Panoramic Neural Radiance Field From a Single Panorama" [S1].
+*   **History:** The method builds upon Instant-NGP [S1] and Stable Diffusion [S1]. It addresses the limitations of single-view NeRF reconstruction by introducing a progressive strategy to handle the ambiguity of unseen regions [S1].
+*   **Usage:** Evaluated on the Replica dataset and in-the-wild indoor scenes [S1].
 
